1
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:08,234
What if you saved $5 a day, starting in January 2010?

2
00:00:08,514 --> 00:00:10,917
Not a fortune. Just the price of one coffee.

3
00:00:11,197 --> 00:00:13,659
Today I'll show you two timelines of the same life.

4
00:00:13,939 --> 00:00:15,935
Same person. Same $5.

5
00:00:16,215 --> 00:00:20,407
And by the end, one of them is sitting on almost $100,000.

6
00:00:21,157 --> 00:00:23,757
Here's the rule. Every day, $5.

7
00:00:24,037 --> 00:00:26,487
That's about $152 a month.

8
00:00:26,767 --> 00:00:28,137
In timeline A, you spend it.

9
00:00:28,417 --> 00:00:34,384
In timeline B, on the first of every month, you put it into an S&P 500 index fund.

10
00:00:34,664 --> 00:00:39,158
It simply owns a small piece of about 500 of the largest US companies.

11
00:00:39,438 --> 00:00:41,376
Nothing fancy. No stock picking.

12
00:00:42,126 --> 00:00:44,135
Timeline A first. It's 2010.

13
00:00:44,415 --> 00:00:47,434
You grab a coffee on the way to work. It tastes great.

14
00:00:47,714 --> 00:00:49,861
You do it again tomorrow. And the day after.

15
00:00:50,141 --> 00:00:54,205
By September 2026, that's about 6,100 coffees.

16
00:00:54,485 --> 00:00:57,991
$30,569, spent.

17
00:00:58,271 --> 00:01:01,673
And what's left of it today? Some good mornings. And zero dollars.

18
00:01:02,423 --> 00:01:05,825
And even if you'd kept that cash under a mattress, it would be shrinking.

19
00:01:06,105 --> 00:01:11,956
Between 2010 and 2023 alone, US consumer prices rose about 41%.

20
00:01:12,236 --> 00:01:15,057
The same banknote buys less every single year.

21
00:01:15,807 --> 00:01:19,499
Now, timeline B. Same coffee money, but invested.

22
00:01:19,779 --> 00:01:21,428
The first years feel pointless.

23
00:01:21,708 --> 00:01:27,385
After two years you've put in about $3,800, and it's worth about $4,100.

24
00:01:27,665 --> 00:01:30,753
A few hundred dollars of growth. Most people quit right here.

25
00:01:31,503 --> 00:01:33,500
But something quietly starts happening.

26
00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:37,519
Your money earns returns, and then those returns earn returns of their own.

27
00:01:37,799 --> 00:01:38,646
That's compounding.

28
00:01:38,926 --> 00:01:45,451
By January 2018, you've put in about $14,800, and it's worth $25,000.

29
00:01:46,201 --> 00:01:49,429
Then, 2020. A pandemic shuts down the world.

30
00:01:49,709 --> 00:01:56,024
In a single month, your account falls from about $33,800 to $27,500.

31
00:01:56,304 --> 00:01:58,951
More than $6,000, gone on paper.

32
00:01:59,231 --> 00:02:00,636
Your stomach drops too.

33
00:02:01,386 --> 00:02:03,882
This is the moment that splits the timeline again.

34
00:02:04,162 --> 00:02:09,782
In one version, you panic, sell everything in April 2020, and keep saving in cash.

35
00:02:10,062 --> 00:02:14,148
By 2026 you'd have about $40,500. Not bad.

36
00:02:14,428 --> 00:02:16,983
But compare it to the version of you that did nothing at all.

37
00:02:17,733 --> 00:02:21,146
That version kept buying. $5 a day, every day.

38
00:02:21,426 --> 00:02:28,775
And it got tested again in 2022, when the account fell from about $52,500 to $43,200.

39
00:02:29,055 --> 00:02:30,680
Again, you kept buying.

40
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:34,072
Every drop meant your $5 bought more shares, on sale.

41
00:02:34,822 --> 00:02:36,668
So. September 2026.

42
00:02:36,948 --> 00:02:41,452
Total money you put in: $30,569.

43
00:02:41,732 --> 00:02:44,647
What it's worth: about $99,000.

44
00:02:44,927 --> 00:02:47,794
Same coffee money. More than three times bigger.

45
00:02:48,074 --> 00:02:51,209
And that's price growth only, without counting dividends.

46
00:02:51,959 --> 00:02:53,793
Okay, but what if you're starting late?

47
00:02:54,073 --> 00:03:00,679
Start in 2015: about $21,400 in, almost $50,000 now.

48
00:03:00,959 --> 00:03:05,557
Start in 2020: about $12,300 in, $20,700 now.

49
00:03:05,837 --> 00:03:12,838
Even starting in January 2022, right before a drop: $8,700 became $13,000.

50
00:03:13,588 --> 00:03:15,480
And the amount matters less than you think.

51
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:19,998
Just $1 a day since 2010 would be worth almost $20,000.

52
00:03:20,278 --> 00:03:22,762
$10 a day? Almost $200,000.

53
00:03:23,512 --> 00:03:26,879
Some people call this the latte factor, and they argue it's a myth.

54
00:03:27,159 --> 00:03:31,188
And they have a point. Cutting coffee won't make anyone rich if rent eats half their paycheck.

55
00:03:31,468 --> 00:03:34,127
The coffee was never the point. The point is the habit:

56
00:03:34,407 --> 00:03:38,784
a small, boring amount, moving every month, for a very long time.

57
00:03:39,534 --> 00:03:42,332
So how would timeline B actually work in real life?

58
00:03:42,612 --> 00:03:47,035
First, make it automatic. A transfer on payday, so you never have to decide.

59
00:03:47,315 --> 00:03:52,261
Second, keep it simple and cheap. Broad index funds usually have very low fees.

60
00:03:52,541 --> 00:03:57,696
And third, don't check it every day. The people who did best in this story were the ones who did nothing.

61
00:03:58,446 --> 00:04:01,615
Now, the honest part. This is not financial advice.

62
00:04:01,895 --> 00:04:06,748
These are real S&P 500 prices, but the past doesn't promise the future.

63
00:04:07,028 --> 00:04:09,652
There have been long stretches when the market went nowhere.

64
00:04:09,932 --> 00:04:15,424
Invest only money you won't need soon, after an emergency fund, and with no expensive debt.

65
00:04:16,174 --> 00:04:19,378
So this isn't really about coffee. Enjoy your coffee.

66
00:04:19,658 --> 00:04:23,222
It's about the two timelines every small habit quietly creates.

67
00:04:23,502 --> 00:04:27,705
Which timeline are you in? Tell me in the comments what you'd do with $5 a day.

68
00:04:27,985 --> 00:04:29,471
See you in the other timeline.
